IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `rsc-dates`
IMPORT `rsc-rules`

-- Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA) -- the questions of a person bringing a claim (the plaintiff) in the Supreme
-- Court of Western Australia: for example a claim for damages for personal injury begun by writ.
--
-- Based on content from the Western Australian Legislation website: "Rules of the Supreme Court 1971", State of Western
-- Australia, licensed under CC BY 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/). BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public
-- use as advice. See NOTES.md.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts only. What the Court decides (whether it allows, is satisfied, orders otherwise) is
-- asked as a fact: "if the Court ...". A yes about the Court means the Court can do it, not that it will. Dates leave out
-- Public Service holidays other than Good Friday, Easter Monday and Christmas Day (fork F2): check a date that falls on
-- one.

§ `Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 -- my questions as a plaintiff`

§§ `Time (O. 3)`

@export What is the last day of a period of days under the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA) (O. 3 rr. 2-4)? The day the period runs from is not counted; for a period under 7 days, weekends, Good Friday, Easter Monday and Christmas Day are not counted; for a pleading or notice of appeal, 24 December to 15 January is not counted; a last day on which the Central Office is closed moves to the next open day if the step needs the office open. Public Service holidays are not computed.
GIVEN `the day it runs from` IS A DATE @desc The day the period runs from, for example the day a document was served on you (ISO date, e.g. 2026-03-15). This day itself is not counted.
      `how many days` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days the rule or order allows, for example 14. Not "clear days".
      `a pleading step` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the step filing, serving or amending a pleading (statement of claim, defence, reply) or filing and serving a notice of appeal?
      `needs the office open` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can the step only be done while the Court's Central Office is open (for example lodging a paper document)? Answer no for a document that must be filed electronically, and for serving a document on the other side.
GIVETH A DATE
`What is the last day of my period?` `the day it runs from` `how many days` `a pleading step` `needs the office open` MEANS
    `O. 3 rr. 2-4 -- the last day` `the day it runs from` `how many days` `a pleading step` `needs the office open`

-- Situation: only where nothing has happened in the action for a year or more.
@export Must I give the other parties notice before I take the next step in my action, because a year or more has passed since the last proceeding (O. 3 r. 7 of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? The notice must be at least one month.
GIVEN `the last proceeding` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the last proceeding in the action (ISO date)? A summons on which no order was made does not count.
      `the day I want to proceed` IS A DATE @desc On what day do you want to take the next step (ISO date)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I give notice of intention to proceed?` `the last proceeding` `the day I want to proceed` MEANS
    `O. 3 r. 7 -- notice of intention to proceed is needed` `the last proceeding` `the day I want to proceed`

@export If I give notice of intention to proceed on this day, what is the earliest day I can take the next step in my action (O. 3 r. 7 of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA): not less than one month's notice)?
GIVEN `the day I give notice` IS A DATE @desc On what day will you give (or did you give) the notice to every other party (ISO date)?
GIVETH A DATE
`What is the earliest day I can proceed after my notice?` `the day I give notice` MEANS
    `O. 3 r. 7 -- the earliest day to proceed after notice` `the day I give notice`

§§ `My writ (O. 5 r. 11, O. 7)`

@export How many days at least must my writ give the defendant to enter an appearance (O. 5 r. 11 of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? In Western Australia it depends on the distance from Perth (10, 16 or 21 days); outside Australia 42 days unless the Court orders otherwise. Not answered for service elsewhere in Australia, which the Service and Execution of Process Act 1992 (Cth) governs.
GIVEN `where it will be served` IS A `Place of service` @desc Where will the writ be served on the defendant: in Western Australia, elsewhere in Australia, or outside Australia?
      `km from Perth` IS A NUMBER @desc If in Western Australia, how many kilometres from Perth is the place of service? Enter 0 otherwise.
      `the Court ordered another time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If outside Australia, has the Court ordered a different time for appearance (O. 10 r. 9)?
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`How many days must my writ give to appear?` `where it will be served` `km from Perth` `the Court ordered another time` MEANS
    `O. 5 r. 11 -- the least time to appear` `where it will be served` `km from Perth` `the Court ordered another time`

@export Until what day is my writ valid for service (O. 7 r. 1(1) of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? A writ is valid for 12 months beginning with the day it was issued, unless the Court extends it.
GIVEN `the day the writ was issued` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the writ issued (sealed) by the Court (ISO date)?
GIVETH A DATE
`Until when is my writ valid for service?` `the day the writ was issued` MEANS
    `O. 7 r. 1(1) -- the last day the writ is valid` `the day the writ was issued`

-- Situation: only where the writ has not yet been served.
@export Can the Court extend the validity of my writ if I apply on this day (O. 7 r. 1(2) of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? Only for a writ not yet served, and the application must be made by the day the writ would expire unless the Court allows a later day.
GIVEN `the day the writ was issued` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the writ issued (ISO date)? Used to work out the day it expires.
      `it has been served` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the writ already been served on the defendant?
      `the day I apply` IS A DATE @desc On what day will you apply (or did you apply) to the Court for the extension (ISO date)?
      `the Court allows a later day` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you apply after the writ expired, has the Court allowed the application to be made late?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Court extend my writ?` `the day the writ was issued` `it has been served` `the day I apply` `the Court allows a later day` MEANS
    `O. 7 r. 1(2) -- the Court can extend the writ` (`O. 7 r. 1(1) -- the last day the writ is valid` `the day the writ was issued`) `it has been served` `the day I apply` `the Court allows a later day`

@export If the Court extends my writ once, what is the latest day the extension can run to (O. 7 r. 1(2) of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA): not more than 12 months at a time, from the day after it would otherwise expire)?
GIVEN `the day it would otherwise expire` IS A DATE @desc On what day would the writ otherwise stop being valid (ISO date)? For a writ never extended, the day before the first anniversary of its issue.
GIVETH A DATE
`What is the latest day one extension can reach?` `the day it would otherwise expire` MEANS
    `O. 7 r. 1(2) -- the latest day one extension can reach` `the day it would otherwise expire`

-- Situation: only where the writ has not been served after 6 months.
@export Can my writ be struck out at a hearing on this day because it has not been served (O. 7 r. 4 of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? Only more than 6 months after issue, where no affidavit of service has been filed and no appearance entered, and the Court is not satisfied there is a good reason it has not been served.
GIVEN `the day the writ was issued` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the writ issued (ISO date)?
      `the day of the hearing` IS A DATE @desc On what day is the show-cause hearing (ISO date)?
      `affidavit of service filed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you filed an affidavit of service of the writ?
      `appearance entered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the defendant entered an appearance?
      `the Court accepts my reason` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the Court satisfied that the writ has not been served for good reason?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my unserved writ be struck out?` `the day the writ was issued` `the day of the hearing` `affidavit of service filed` `appearance entered` `the Court accepts my reason` MEANS
    `O. 7 r. 4 -- the writ can be struck out` `the day the writ was issued` `the day of the hearing` `affidavit of service filed` `appearance entered` `the Court accepts my reason`

§§ `Service and default of appearance (O. 9 r. 1, O. 13)`

@export By what day must the person who served my writ personally indorse the service details on the sealed copy, so that I can later enter default judgment (O. 9 r. 1(4) of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA): within 3 days after service, not counting weekends, Good Friday, Easter Monday or Christmas Day)?
GIVEN `the day the writ was served` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the writ served on the defendant (ISO date)?
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must service be indorsed on the writ?` `the day the writ was served` MEANS
    `O. 9 r. 1(4) -- the last day to indorse service` `the day the writ was served`

@export What is the last day for the defendant to enter an appearance to my writ (O. 5 r. 11(4), O. 3 rr. 2, 4 of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? The day of service is not counted; a last day on which the Central Office is closed moves to the next open day.
GIVEN `the day the writ was served` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the writ served on the defendant (ISO date)? If the defendant's lawyer accepted service, the day they indorsed the writ.
      `days given to appear` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days does the writ give the defendant to enter an appearance (for example 10)?
GIVETH A DATE
`What is the last day for the defendant to appear?` `the day the writ was served` `days given to appear` MEANS
    `O. 5 r. 11, O. 3 -- the last day to appear` `the day the writ was served` `days given to appear`

-- Situation: only where the defendant has not entered an appearance.
@export If the defendant has not entered an appearance, what judgment can I enter on this day without a trial (O. 13 and O. 9 r. 1(4) of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? A claim for damages for personal injury is a claim for unliquidated damages: judgment is entered and the damages are assessed by a master. Service must first be proved.
GIVEN `kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What does the writ claim? A personal injury claim is "unliquidated damages only"; a fixed sum owed is "a debt or other liquidated demand only".
      `the day the writ was served` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the writ served on the defendant (ISO date)?
      `days given to appear` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days does the writ give the defendant to enter an appearance?
      `the day I would enter it` IS A DATE @desc On what day do you want to enter judgment (ISO date)?
      `the defendant has appeared` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the defendant entered an appearance (even late)?
      `the solicitor accepted service` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the defendant's lawyer indorse the writ accepting service for the defendant?
      `indorsed within 3 days` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If served personally: did the person who served it indorse the day, place and person served on the sealed copy within 3 days after service?
      `affidavit of service filed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If served personally: have you filed an affidavit proving service and the indorsement?
GIVETH A `Default judgment`
`What default judgment can I enter?` `kind of claim` `the day the writ was served` `days given to appear` `the day I would enter it` `the defendant has appeared` `the solicitor accepted service` `indorsed within 3 days` `affidavit of service filed` MEANS
    `O. 13 -- the default judgment I can enter` `kind of claim` (`O. 5 r. 11, O. 3 -- the last day to appear` `the day the writ was served` `days given to appear`) `the day I would enter it` `the defendant has appeared` (`O. 9 r. 1(4), O. 13 r. 1(3) -- service is proved` `the solicitor accepted service` `indorsed within 3 days` `affidavit of service filed`)

§§ `Pleadings and summary judgment (O. 14 r. 1, O. 20)`

-- Situation: only where the statement of claim was not indorsed on the writ.
@export By what day must I serve my statement of claim on the defendant (O. 20 r. 1 of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? Before 14 days after the defendant enters an appearance run out, not counting 24 December to 15 January. Not needed if the statement of claim is indorsed on the writ or the Court gives leave.
GIVEN `the day the defendant appeared` IS A DATE @desc On what day did the defendant enter an appearance (ISO date)?
      `indorsed on the writ` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the statement of claim indorsed on (written on) the writ itself?
      `the Court gave leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Court given leave not to serve a statement of claim in this way?
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`By when must I serve my statement of claim?` `the day the defendant appeared` `indorsed on the writ` `the Court gave leave` MEANS
    IF `indorsed on the writ` OR `the Court gave leave`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`O. 20 r. 1 -- the last day to serve my statement of claim` `the day the defendant appeared`)

@export Did I serve my statement of claim in time (O. 20 r. 1 of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? Service with the writ, or after it and before 14 days after the defendant's appearance run out (24 December to 15 January not counted), is in time.
GIVEN `the day the defendant appeared` IS A DATE @desc On what day did the defendant enter an appearance (ISO date)?
      `the day I served it` IS A DATE @desc On what day did you serve (or will you serve) the statement of claim (ISO date)? It must be after the writ was served or with it.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Did I serve my statement of claim in time?` `the day the defendant appeared` `the day I served it` MEANS
    `O. 20 r. 1 -- my statement of claim was served in time` `the day the defendant appeared` `the day I served it`

@export By what day must the defendant serve a defence on me (O. 20 r. 4 of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? 14 days after the later of the end of the time to appear and the day my statement of claim was served, not counting 24 December to 15 January. Not answered where I served a summary judgment summons first: then the Court's order sets the time.
GIVEN `the day the writ was served` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the writ served on the defendant (ISO date)?
      `days given to appear` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days does the writ give the defendant to enter an appearance?
      `the day my statement of claim was served` IS A DATE @desc On what day was your statement of claim served on the defendant (ISO date)? If indorsed on the writ, the day the writ was served.
      `a summary judgment summons came first` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you serve a summons for summary judgment (O. 14) on the defendant before they served a defence?
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`By when must the defendant serve a defence?` `the day the writ was served` `days given to appear` `the day my statement of claim was served` `a summary judgment summons came first` MEANS
    `O. 20 r. 4 -- the last day for the defence` (`O. 5 r. 11, O. 3 -- the last day to appear` `the day the writ was served` `days given to appear`) `the day my statement of claim was served` `a summary judgment summons came first`

@export By what day must I serve my reply to the defendant's defence, if I need one (O. 20 r. 5(4) of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? Before 14 days after the defence was served on me run out, not counting 24 December to 15 January.
GIVEN `the day the defence was served` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the defence served on you (ISO date)?
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I serve my reply?` `the day the defence was served` MEANS
    `O. 20 r. 5(4) -- the last day for my reply` `the day the defence was served`

@export On what day do the pleadings in my action close (O. 20 r. 20(1) of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? At the end of the 14th day after service of the reply (or defence to counterclaim), or of the defence if neither was served. A time extended by order or consent moves this (r. 20(2)).
GIVEN `the day of the last pleading served` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the reply served (or, if no reply, the defence to counterclaim; or, if neither, the defence) (ISO date)?
GIVETH A DATE
`When do the pleadings close?` `the day of the last pleading served` MEANS
    `O. 20 r. 20 -- the day the pleadings close` `the day of the last pleading served`

@export By what day can I apply for summary judgment without the Court's leave, on the ground that the defendant has no defence (O. 14 r. 1 of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? Within 21 days after the defendant's appearance; later only with leave. The statement of claim must have been served.
GIVEN `the day the defendant appeared` IS A DATE @desc On what day did the defendant enter an appearance (ISO date)?
GIVETH A DATE
`By when can I apply for summary judgment without leave?` `the day the defendant appeared` MEANS
    `O. 14 r. 1 -- the last day to apply without leave` `the day the defendant appeared`

§§ `Offers to settle (O. 24A)`

-- Situation: only where the defendant has made me an offer of compromise under O. 24A.
@export By what day must I serve a written acknowledgment that I received the defendant's offer of compromise (O. 24A r. 3(4) of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? Within 3 days after service, not counting weekends, Good Friday, Easter Monday or Christmas Day.
GIVEN `the day the offer was served on me` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the offer served on you (ISO date)?
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I acknowledge the offer?` `the day the offer was served on me` MEANS
    `O. 24A r. 3(4) -- the last day to acknowledge` `the day the offer was served on me`

@export Did I acknowledge the defendant's offer of compromise in time (O. 24A r. 3(4) of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))?
GIVEN `the day the offer was served on me` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the offer served on you (ISO date)?
      `the day I acknowledged` IS A DATE @desc On what day did you serve your written acknowledgment of receipt (ISO date)?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Did I acknowledge the offer in time?` `the day the offer was served on me` `the day I acknowledged` MEANS
    `O. 24A r. 3(4) -- I acknowledged in time` `the day the offer was served on me` `the day I acknowledged`

@export Can I accept the defendant's offer of compromise on this day (O. 24A r. 3 of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? An offer is open for the time it states, which cannot be less than 28 days, or 28 days if it states none; and never after the judge begins to sum up to a jury or to give the decision.
GIVEN `the day the offer was made` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the offer served on you (ISO date)? An offer is made by serving it.
      `days the offer is open` IS A NUMBER @desc For how many days does the offer say it is open? Enter 0 if it does not say.
      `the day I accept` IS A DATE @desc On what day will you serve your written notice of acceptance (ISO date)?
      `the judge has begun the decision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the judge (or master) begun to sum up to a jury, or to give the decision or reasons for the final judgment?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I accept the offer?` `the day the offer was made` `days the offer is open` `the day I accept` `the judge has begun the decision` MEANS
    `O. 24A r. 3 -- I can accept the offer` `the day the offer was made` `days the offer is open` `the day I accept` `the judge has begun the decision`

-- Situation: only after I accepted an offer to pay me a sum.
@export Has the defendant paid the sum in an offer I accepted in time (O. 24A r. 4 of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? Unless the offer says otherwise, the sum is to be paid within 28 days after acceptance. No answer while that time is still running and the sum is unpaid.
GIVEN `the day I accepted` IS A DATE @desc On what day did you accept the offer (ISO date)?
      `days the offer gives to pay` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days after acceptance does the offer give for payment? Enter 0 if it does not say (then 28).
      `it has been paid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the sum been paid?
      `the day it was paid` IS A DATE @desc If paid, on what day (ISO date)? Any date if not paid.
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (ISO date).
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was the accepted offer paid in time?` `the day I accepted` `days the offer gives to pay` `it has been paid` `the day it was paid` `today` MEANS
    `O. 24A r. 4 -- the sum was paid in time` `the day I accepted` `days the offer gives to pay` `it has been paid` `the day it was paid` `today`

@export Can I withdraw my acceptance of an offer to pay me a sum, on this day (O. 24A r. 5 of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? Yes if the sum was not paid into Court within 28 days after acceptance, or if the Court gives leave. Not answered where the sum was paid to you directly in time, which the rule does not address.
GIVEN `the day I accepted` IS A DATE @desc On what day did you accept the offer (ISO date)?
      `how it was paid` IS A `How the sum was paid` @desc Has the sum been paid: not paid, paid into Court, or paid to you (or your lawyer) directly?
      `the day it was paid` IS A DATE @desc If paid, on what day (ISO date)? Any date if not paid.
      `the day I withdraw` IS A DATE @desc On what day would you serve the notice of withdrawal (ISO date)?
      `the Court gives leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Court given you leave to withdraw the acceptance?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I withdraw my acceptance?` `the day I accepted` `how it was paid` `the day it was paid` `the day I withdraw` `the Court gives leave` MEANS
    `O. 24A r. 5 -- I can withdraw my acceptance` `the day I accepted` `how it was paid` `the day it was paid` `the day I withdraw` `the Court gives leave`

@export If an offer of compromise of a sum of money was not accepted and judgment has been given, what costs order follows (O. 24A r. 10 of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? If my offer is beaten or matched by the judgment I get my costs, on the indemnity basis from the offer if the defendant was unreasonable not to accept; if I do no better than the defendant's offer, I pay the defendant's costs after it. Interest for the time after the offer is left out of the comparison. Not answered where the Court orders otherwise.
GIVEN `who made the offer` IS A `Who made the offer` @desc Who made the offer: you or the defendant?
      `the sum offered` IS A NUMBER @desc The sum the offer was for, in dollars.
      `the judgment` IS A NUMBER @desc The sum of the judgment you obtained, in dollars, including any interest (0 if you lost).
      `interest after the offer` IS A NUMBER @desc How much of that judgment is interest (or damages in the nature of interest) for the time after the day the offer was made, in dollars?
      `made on or after the first day of trial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the offer made on or after the first day of the trial?
      `the offeror was willing and able` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the Court satisfied that whoever made the offer was at all times willing and able to carry it out?
      `not accepting was unreasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the Court satisfied that the other side's failure to accept the offer was unreasonable?
      `justice requires otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the Court find that the interests of justice require costs not on the indemnity basis?
      `the Court orders otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Court ordered otherwise as to these costs?
GIVETH A MAYBE `Costs on the offer`
`What costs follow the offer?` `who made the offer` `the sum offered` `the judgment` `interest after the offer` `made on or after the first day of trial` `the offeror was willing and able` `not accepting was unreasonable` `justice requires otherwise` `the Court orders otherwise` MEANS
    `O. 24A r. 10 -- the costs that follow the offer` `who made the offer` `the sum offered` `the judgment` `interest after the offer` `made on or after the first day of trial` `the offeror was willing and able` `not accepting was unreasonable` `justice requires otherwise` `the Court orders otherwise`

-- Situation: only where an accepted offer was not carried out by the defendant.
@export If the defendant does not carry out an offer of compromise that was accepted, am I entitled to an order striking out the defence and to judgment (O. 24A rr. 8, 9 of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? Not where the Court finds special cause, nor where defendants jointly liable with rights of contribution were not all covered by the offer. You may instead apply for whatever judgment you are entitled to.
GIVEN `the defendant has failed to comply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the defendant failed to comply with the terms of the accepted offer?
      `the Court finds special cause` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Court, for special cause, ordered otherwise?
      `jointly liable defendants` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are two or more defendants said to be jointly (or jointly and severally) liable to you, with rights of contribution or indemnity between them?
      `the offer covers all of them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so, was the offer to settle the claim against all of those defendants (and, if made by two or more of them, are they jointly liable for the whole sum)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I have the defence struck out for breach of the accepted offer?` `the defendant has failed to comply` `the Court finds special cause` `jointly liable defendants` `the offer covers all of them` MEANS
    `O. 24A rr. 8, 9 -- I am entitled to have the defence struck out` `the defendant has failed to comply` `the Court finds special cause` `jointly liable defendants` `the offer covers all of them`

§§ `Ending my action (O. 23)`

@export Can I discontinue my action, or withdraw part of my claim, by written notice without the Court's leave (O. 23 r. 2 of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? Yes before I receive the defence, or after it before I take any other step; otherwise leave is needed.
GIVEN `I have received the defence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you received the defendant's defence?
      `I have taken another step since` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Since receiving it, have you taken any other step in the action?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Can I discontinue without leave?` `I have received the defence` `I have taken another step since` MEANS
    `O. 23 r. 2 -- I can discontinue by notice` `I have received the defence` `I have taken another step since`

@export If I discontinue my action, must I pay the defendant's costs (O. 23 r. 2(1) of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? Yes on a discontinuance by notice (the costs of the action, or of the part withdrawn, taxed); with leave, the Court sets the terms.
GIVEN `by notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Will you discontinue by written notice without the Court's leave (rather than by the Court's order)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I pay the defendant's costs if I discontinue?` `by notice` MEANS
    `O. 23 r. 2(1) -- I must pay the defendant's costs` `by notice`

@export If I discontinued an action and bring a new one, can the new action be stayed until I pay the costs of the first (O. 23 rr. 2(2), 4 of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? Discontinuing is not itself a defence to a new action, but the Court may stay a new action on the same cause of action until those costs are paid.
GIVEN `same cause of action` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the new action for the same, or substantially the same, cause of action?
      `I have paid those costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you paid the costs of the discontinued action?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my new action be stayed until I pay the old costs?` `same cause of action` `I have paid those costs` MEANS
    `O. 23 r. 4 -- my new action can be stayed` `same cause of action` `I have paid those costs`

§§ `Security for costs (O. 25)`

-- Situation: only where the defendant seeks security for costs.
@export Can the Court order me, as plaintiff, to give security for the defendant's costs (O. 25 rr. 1-3 of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? The Court has a general discretion, with listed grounds, but no order may be made merely because I am poor or could not pay costs. A yes means the Court can, not that it will.
GIVEN `why` IS A `Why security is sought` @desc Why does the defendant say you should give security? Choose "only that I am poor or could not pay the costs" if that is the only reason.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I be ordered to give security for costs?` `why` MEANS
    `O. 25 rr. 1, 2 -- the Court can order me to give security` `why`

@export Is my action on hold (stayed) until I give the security for costs the Court ordered (O. 25 r. 6 of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))?
GIVEN `security was ordered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Court ordered you to give security for costs?
      `I have given it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you given the security in the form ordered?
      `the Court ordered no stay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Court ordered that the action not be stayed?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my action stayed until I give security?` `security was ordered` `I have given it` `the Court ordered no stay` MEANS
    `O. 25 r. 6 -- my action is stayed` `security was ordered` `I have given it` `the Court ordered no stay`

§§ `Medical examination (O. 28 r. 1)`

-- Situation: only where my physical or mental condition is in issue, as in a personal injury claim.
@export Must I attend a medical examination the defendant asks for (O. 28 r. 1 of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? Yes where my condition is material and I am served with a notice naming the time and place, with a doctor they provide and pay; if I refuse, the Court can stay my claim or strike out a pleading. My own medical adviser may attend.
GIVEN `my condition is material` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your physical or mental condition material to the case (as it is in a personal injury claim)?
      `notice served` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the defendant served you with a notice to attend an examination at a specified time and place?
      `their doctor, at their cost` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the examining doctor a medical practitioner provided and paid for by the defendant?
      `the Court ordered I need not attend` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you objected, has the Court ordered that you need not attend?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I attend the defendant's medical examination?` `my condition is material` `notice served` `their doctor, at their cost` `the Court ordered I need not attend` MEANS
    `O. 28 r. 1 -- I must submit to the examination` `my condition is material` `notice served` `their doctor, at their cost` `the Court ordered I need not attend`

@export Must the defendant pay me a reasonable sum for my travel and other expenses of their medical examination, including my own medical adviser's attendance (O. 28 r. 1(2) of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? Yes, on demand.
GIVEN `I have demanded it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you demanded the payment from the defendant?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the defendant pay my examination expenses?` `I have demanded it` MEANS
    `O. 28 r. 1(2) -- my expenses must be paid` `I have demanded it`

@export Can I get a Court order for a copy of the defendant's medical report on me, or a further and better report (O. 28 r. 1(4), (5) of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? The defendant must serve me a full and true copy; I can get an order if they are a week late or I say the report is incomplete.
GIVEN `the copy has been served` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the defendant served on you a copy of the examining doctor's report?
      `a week has passed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the defendant had the report for more than a week without serving you a copy?
      `I say it is incomplete` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you say the report is insufficient or incomplete?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I get an order for the medical report?` `the copy has been served` `a week has passed` `I say it is incomplete` MEANS
    `O. 28 r. 1(5) -- I can get an order for the report` `the copy has been served` `a week has passed` `I say it is incomplete`

§§ `Children and people who cannot manage their affairs (O. 70)`

-- Situation: only for a claimant under 18, or under guardianship or administration, or declared incapable.
@export Must my claim be brought by a next friend (a litigation representative acting through a lawyer) (O. 70 rr. 1, 2 of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? Yes for a child under 18, a represented person under the Guardianship and Administration Act 1990, or a person the Court declared incapable, unless a judge permits a child to sue alone.
GIVEN `I am under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you under 18?
      `I am a represented person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a guardian or administrator been appointed for you under the Guardianship and Administration Act 1990 with authority to conduct or settle proceedings?
      `declared incapable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Court declared you incapable of managing your affairs in these proceedings because of mental illness, defect or infirmity?
      `a judge permitted me to sue alone` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a judge ordered that you (as a child) may sue without a next friend?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Must I sue by a next friend?` `I am under 18` `I am a represented person` `declared incapable` `a judge permitted me to sue alone` MEANS
    `O. 70 r. 2 -- I must sue by a next friend` `I am under 18` `I am a represented person` `declared incapable` `a judge permitted me to sue alone`

@export Is a settlement of my claim valid without the Court's approval (O. 70 rr. 10, 11 of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? For a child or other person under disability, no settlement, compromise or acceptance of money paid into court in proceedings is valid unless the Court approves it. Before proceedings begin, approval may be sought by originating summons; the rules do not say an unapproved one is invalid.
GIVEN `I am under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you under 18?
      `I am a represented person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a guardian or administrator been appointed for you under the Guardianship and Administration Act 1990 with authority to conduct or settle proceedings?
      `declared incapable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Court declared you incapable of managing your affairs in these proceedings?
      `proceedings have begun` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have proceedings on the claim been commenced in the Court?
      `it is an appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the settlement of an appeal to the Court of Appeal?
      `the Court approved it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Court approved the settlement?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my settlement valid without the Court's approval?` `I am under 18` `I am a represented person` `declared incapable` `proceedings have begun` `it is an appeal` `the Court approved it` MEANS
    `O. 70 rr. 10, 11 -- the settlement is valid` (`O. 70 r. 1 -- I am a person under disability` `I am under 18` `I am a represented person` `declared incapable`) `proceedings have begun` `it is an appeal` `the Court approved it`

@export Where will money recovered for me in the proceedings be paid (O. 70 r. 12 of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? For a person under disability, to the Public Trustee to invest for them, unless the Court orders otherwise.
GIVEN `I am under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you under 18?
      `I am a represented person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a guardian or administrator been appointed for you under the Guardianship and Administration Act 1990 with authority to conduct or settle proceedings?
      `declared incapable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Court declared you incapable of managing your affairs in these proceedings?
      `the Court ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Court ordered the money to be paid or invested some other way?
GIVETH A `Where the money goes`
`Where will the money recovered for me be paid?` `I am under 18` `I am a represented person` `declared incapable` `the Court ordered otherwise` MEANS
    `O. 70 r. 12 -- where my money goes` (`O. 70 r. 1 -- I am a person under disability` `I am under 18` `I am a represented person` `declared incapable`) `the Court ordered otherwise`

§§ `Inactive cases (O. 4A Division 5)`

-- Situation: only where no step has been taken in the case for a long time.
@export From what day is my case taken to be inactive if no party takes a procedural step (O. 4A r. 24 of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? After 12 months without a procedural step by any party, unless the case manager orders otherwise. It then goes on the Inactive Cases List.
GIVEN `the last procedural step` IS A DATE @desc On what day did any party last take a procedural step in the case (ISO date)?
GIVETH A DATE
`From when is my case taken to be inactive?` `the last procedural step` MEANS
    `O. 4A r. 24 -- the day the case is taken to be inactive` `the last procedural step`

-- Situation: only where the case has been put on the Inactive Cases List.
@export Has my case been taken to be dismissed for want of prosecution (O. 4A r. 28 of the Rules of the Supreme Court 1971 (WA))? A case on the Inactive Cases List for 6 continuous months after notice is dismissed; so is a case taken off the list in which no procedural step follows in the next 6 months.
GIVEN `the day notice was given` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the notice that the case is on the Inactive Cases List given (the last day, if parties got it on different days) (ISO date)?
      `taken off the list` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Court ordered the case taken off the Inactive Cases List?
      `the day taken off` IS A DATE @desc If so, on what day was that order made (ISO date)? Any date if not.
      `a step since taken` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If taken off, has any procedural step (other than an application to dismiss) been taken since?
      `the day of the first step since` IS A DATE @desc If so, on what day was the first such step (ISO date)? Any date if not.
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (ISO date).
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Has my case been dismissed for inactivity?` `the day notice was given` `taken off the list` `the day taken off` `a step since taken` `the day of the first step since` `today` MEANS
    `O. 4A r. 28 -- the case is taken to have been dismissed` `the day notice was given` `taken off the list` `the day taken off` `a step since taken` `the day of the first step since` `today`
